# ===========================================================================
# ATHERA — single entry point.
#
# Adapted from the platform repo's caddy/Caddyfile.multitenant on 2026-09-01.
# Made environment-driven on 2026-09-02: every hostname and the TLS mode now
# come from .env, so a deployment host differs from a workstation by
# configuration rather than by a patched copy of this file.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS AT ALL, and it is not TLS convenience.
#
# Odoo serves a database only if `dbfilter` matches it. With one database the
# anchored form ^bct$ is enough. With two, a filter naming both was MEASURED to
# serve NEITHER: Odoo cannot choose, so /web/login answers
# `303 -> /web/database/selector`, and list_db=False disables that selector.
# A wider regex does not admit a second database; it breaks the first one.
#
# The only thing that makes more than one database reachable is a per-request
# way to say WHICH — and in Odoo that is the Host header, through dbfilter's
# %d. So the reverse proxy is not decoration in front of a working stack; it is
# the mechanism that makes multi-tenancy work at all.
#
# %d IS THE FIRST LABEL OF THE HOST, not the whole thing. That is the one
# subtlety here. `odoo.<domain>` would give %d = "odoo" and look
# for a database of that name. So that route REWRITES the upstream Host to
# `<ATHERA_ADMIN_DB>.<domain>`, which makes %d resolve to exactly the right
# database. Tenant routes need no such rewrite: their hostname's first label
# already IS the slug.
#
# TWO VARIABLES DECIDE THE ENVIRONMENT, and nothing else in this file does:
#
#   ATHERA_DOMAIN   athera.localhost on a workstation, the real domain in
#                   production. compose/odoo.yml already builds its network
#                   aliases from the same variable, so the browser-facing
#                   routes here and the in-network RPC names cannot drift
#                   apart — which they silently could while this file
#                   hardcoded its hostnames.
#   ATHERA_TLS      `internal` for a local CA, or an EMAIL ADDRESS to turn on
#                   ACME with that address as the account. `tls <email>` is
#                   Caddy's own syntax for exactly that, so moving from a local
#                   certificate to Let's Encrypt is one .env value and no edit
#                   here.
#
# DO NOT point ATHERA_TLS at an email before the DNS A records exist and have
# propagated. Let's Encrypt rate-limits failed validations (5 per hostname per
# hour), so a Caddy that starts first spends that budget and is then locked out
# at the moment DNS becomes correct.
#
# EVERY HOSTNAME BELOW NEEDS A DNS RECORD in production. Currently:
#   @  www  insight  admin  odoo  auth  bct  acme
#
# HOSTS FILE, workstation only. Windows does not resolve *.localhost
# automatically. Add:
#     127.0.0.1  athera.localhost
#     127.0.0.1  admin.athera.localhost
#     127.0.0.1  odoo.athera.localhost
#     127.0.0.1  insight.athera.localhost
#     127.0.0.1  bct.athera.localhost
#     127.0.0.1  <slug>.athera.localhost      per provisioned client
# Anything scripted can skip that with `curl -H 'Host: ...'`, which is how the
# verification in scripts/ tests these routes without touching the host file.
#
# NO WILDCARD ROUTE, deliberately. Every hostname below is written out. A
# wildcard `*.<domain> -> odoo` plus dbfilter ^%d$ would make every database in
# the cluster reachable by guessing its name, which is precisely the exposure
# contract 04 s8 asks to be reviewed rather than assumed.
# ===========================================================================
{
	# Loopback only. On a workstation this host also runs odoo19-platform-*,
	# odoo19-analytics-* and smart-warga-*; an admin endpoint on 0.0.0.0 would
	# be reachable from the LAN alongside them. On a VPS it would be reachable
	# from the internet, which is worse.
	admin 127.0.0.1:2019

	# A local CA, so https works without a certificate warning being trained
	# out of people. Trust the root once from
	# data/caddy/pki/authorities/local/root.crt
	# Unused when ATHERA_TLS names an email — issuance then goes to ACME.
	pki {
		ca local {
			name "ATHERA Local CA"
		}
	}
}

# The TLS policy, in one place. Every site imports this rather than naming a
# mode of its own, so no route can end up on a different issuer than its
# siblings by being edited alone.
(site_tls) {
	tls {$ATHERA_TLS:internal}
}

# Shared reverse-proxy headers. Odoo runs with proxy_mode=True, which makes it
# TRUST X-Forwarded-*; that setting was already on in this repo while nothing
# sat in front of Odoo, which meant a client could forge them. It is correct
# only now, and only because these are set here on every proxied route.
(odoo_headers) {
	header_up Host {host}
	header_up X-Real-IP {remote_host}
	header_up X-Forwarded-For {remote_host}
	header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {scheme}
}

# Headers for a non-Odoo upstream: the Host passes through unchanged because
# nothing downstream selects a database from it.
(app_headers) {
	header_up Host {host}
	header_up X-Real-IP {remote_host}
	header_up X-Forwarded-For {remote_host}
	header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {scheme}
}

# ----- Public site -------------------------------------------------------
# The diagram's top-left. Pages come from cms.page in the control-plane
# database, so "Company Profile / Product / ETC." are rows rather than routes.
{$ATHERA_DOMAIN:athera.localhost} {
	encode zstd gzip
	import site_tls

	handle /onboarding/* {
		# The public intake controller lives in Odoo (custom_onboarding_journey),
		# so the marketing site's form has a live endpoint before the site does.
		#
		# THE HOST'S FIRST LABEL IS THE CONTROL-PLANE DATABASE, NOT ${ODOO_DB_NAME}.
		# It used to be the latter, which resolved to a CLIENT's database, and that
		# is where every lead submitted through this URL landed. Three things were
		# wrong with it, in ascending order of how hard they are to undo:
		#
		#   1. ATHERA's own sales leads sat inside a client's database - a database
		#      that gets backed up, restored, and one day handed over.
		#   2. Nothing could read them. hub-portal's DSN points at the control
		#      plane and nowhere else, so those rows had no route to a human.
		#   3. The website's own form already posts to the control plane. Two
		#      destinations means half the leads vanish with nobody noticing that
		#      half of them are missing.
		#
		# ${ATHERA_ADMIN_MODULES} lists custom_onboarding_journey; the control
		# plane was always the declared home. This line was the one that strayed.
		reverse_proxy odoo:8069 {
			header_up Host "{$ATHERA_ADMIN_DB:athera_admin}.{$ATHERA_DOMAIN:athera.localhost}"
			header_up X-Forwarded-Host "{$ATHERA_ADMIN_DB:athera_admin}.{$ATHERA_DOMAIN:athera.localhost}"
			# X-FORWARDED-FOR IS PASSED THROUGH, NOT REWRITTEN.
			#
			# It used to be `{remote_host}`, and `{remote_host}` here is not the
			# visitor: it is the previous hop, the host-level Caddy, identical for
			# every request that has ever arrived. Odoo runs behind ProxyFix with
			# x_for=1, and werkzeug's ProxyFix takes `values[-1]` -- the RIGHTMOST
			# entry, i.e. whatever this line writes. So the per-IP rate limit was
			# reading one constant address for the entire internet: not five
			# submissions per hour per visitor, but five per hour for everyone
			# together, and the sixth honest visitor of the hour was turned away.
			#
			# The visitor's address is already in the header when it gets here.
			# The host-level Caddy SETS it from its own {remote_host}, discarding
			# anything the client tried to supply, so this value is not forgeable
			# and this hop must leave it exactly as it found it. Letting Caddy
			# append its own peer would be just as wrong -- the appended entry
			# becomes the rightmost, and we are back to one bucket.
			header_up X-Forwarded-For {http.request.header.X-Forwarded-For}
			header_up X-Real-IP {http.request.header.X-Forwarded-For}
			header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {scheme}
		}

		# An unauthenticated writer does not get to choose how many bytes the
		# control plane parses. Odoo caps the STORED payload, but only after
		# deserialising whatever arrived; this cap happens before the request
		# reaches a Python process at all.
		request_body {
			max_size 256KB
		}
	}

	handle {
		reverse_proxy marketing-site:3000 {
			import app_headers
		}
	}

	log {
		output stdout
		# `filter` wrapping json, NOT plain json. The SSO ticket travels in a query
		# string, so the edge writes it verbatim into `request>uri` -- and the edge is
		# the only place that records it on the same line as the client's real address
		# (`X-Forwarded-For`). Caddy already redacts `Set-Cookie` here, but that is its
		# built-in default and it does not extend to the URI; this has to be asked for.
		#
		# Applied to every site block rather than only the one that receives tickets:
		# the filter is inert where the parameter never appears, and a route that moves
		# later must not quietly reopen this.
		#
		# This stops the ticket being WRITTEN here. It does not touch the `Referer`
		# header or the client's browser history, and the Odoo access log keeps its own
		# copy. Central log shipping is filtered separately, in promtail.
		format filter {
			wrap json
			fields {
				request>uri query {
					replace ticket REDACTED
				}
			}
		}
		level INFO
	}
}

# ----- www -> apex -------------------------------------------------------
# A redirect rather than a second copy of the site: one canonical origin keeps
# cookies, CSP and absolute URLs from having two valid spellings. In production
# `www` is a CNAME to the apex — RFC 1034 forbids a name holding both a CNAME
# and an A record, so it cannot be an A record there and does not need to be.
www.{$ATHERA_DOMAIN:athera.localhost} {
	import site_tls
	redir https://{$ATHERA_DOMAIN:athera.localhost}{uri} permanent
}

# ----- Super Admin CMS ---------------------------------------------------
# The console, since hub-portal landed. It gates on the is_super_admin claim
# the login gateway mints, signs to the orchestrator server-side, and edits the
# CMS the public site reads.
admin.{$ATHERA_DOMAIN:athera.localhost} {
	encode zstd gzip
	import site_tls

	reverse_proxy hub-portal:3000 {
		import app_headers
	}

	log {
		output stdout
		# `filter` wrapping json, NOT plain json. The SSO ticket travels in a query
		# string, so the edge writes it verbatim into `request>uri` -- and the edge is
		# the only place that records it on the same line as the client's real address
		# (`X-Forwarded-For`). Caddy already redacts `Set-Cookie` here, but that is its
		# built-in default and it does not extend to the URI; this has to be asked for.
		#
		# Applied to every site block rather than only the one that receives tickets:
		# the filter is inert where the parameter never appears, and a route that moves
		# later must not quietly reopen this.
		#
		# This stops the ticket being WRITTEN here. It does not touch the `Referer`
		# header or the client's browser history, and the Odoo access log keeps its own
		# copy. Central log shipping is filtered separately, in promtail.
		format filter {
			wrap json
			fields {
				request>uri query {
					replace ticket REDACTED
				}
			}
		}
		level INFO
	}
}

# ----- The admin database's Odoo UI --------------------------------------
# Still reachable, and still needed: tenant.registry, custom_hub_console and
# the onboarding journey are Odoo models, and hub-portal covers the lifecycle
# rather than replacing the whole console. The Host rewrite is the trick
# described in the header — %d is the FIRST LABEL, so `odoo` would look
# for a database of that name.
odoo.{$ATHERA_DOMAIN:athera.localhost} {
	encode zstd gzip
	import site_tls

	# Public pricing at the edge — served BEFORE the gate and before any Odoo Host
	# rewrite, so a visitor with no session can still read what Odoo costs. It never
	# enters Odoo and never touches %d/dbfilter.
	handle /harga* {
		rewrite * /harga/odoo
		reverse_proxy marketing-site:3000 {
			import app_headers
		}
	}

	handle {
		# THE GATE (brief 08, cutover 2026-09-03). Every request asks the gateway one
		# question: which database is this for? A signed route token answers it, and the
		# entitlement behind that token is re-read on each request — so a cancelled
		# subscription closes this door in seconds rather than at the end of a session.
		#
		# WHY forward_auth AND NOT A COOKIE CADDY READS ITSELF. Odoo picks its database
		# from the first label of Host, so Host must be rewritten BEFORE Odoo sees the
		# request, and Caddy cannot read Odoo's session cookie to learn which database
		# that is. A plain cookie naming the database would be editable by the visitor
		# and would hand back the database selector through the back door. The gateway
		# signs the route token; that signature is the whole difference.
		#
		# A browser with no session is answered with a 303 to the gateway's login page
		# (login-gateway returns it; forward_auth copies any non-2xx straight through).
		# A JSON client is answered 401. Only the presentation differs.
		forward_auth login-gateway:8080 {
			uri /auth/sso/route
			# Only this header is copied back onto the request. Copying more would let
			# the gateway inject arbitrary headers into Odoo.
			copy_headers X-Athera-Db
		}

		reverse_proxy odoo:8069 {
			# BOTH headers, and that is the whole fix. Rewriting Host alone was
			# measured to leave this route on 303 -> /web/database/selector while a
			# direct request carrying the same Host answered 200. odoo runs with
			# proxy_mode=True and prefers X-Forwarded-Host when deciding what %d is,
			# and Caddy sets X-Forwarded-Host from the ORIGINAL request — so it said
			# "odoo" and looked for a database of that name. Overriding only the header
			# a human thinks of is how this fails silently.
			#
			# The database is no longer a fixed ATHERA_ADMIN_DB: it comes from the
			# signed token, which is what lets one hostname serve the console AND each
			# client's own database.
			header_up Host "{http.request.header.X-Athera-Db}.{$ATHERA_DOMAIN:athera.localhost}"
			header_up X-Forwarded-Host "{http.request.header.X-Athera-Db}.{$ATHERA_DOMAIN:athera.localhost}"
			header_up X-Real-IP {remote_host}
			header_up X-Forwarded-For {remote_host}
			header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {scheme}
		}
	}

	log {
		output stdout
		# `filter` wrapping json, NOT plain json. The SSO ticket travels in a query
		# string, so the edge writes it verbatim into `request>uri` -- and the edge is
		# the only place that records it on the same line as the client's real address
		# (`X-Forwarded-For`). Caddy already redacts `Set-Cookie` here, but that is its
		# built-in default and it does not extend to the URI; this has to be asked for.
		#
		# Applied to every site block rather than only the one that receives tickets:
		# the filter is inert where the parameter never appears, and a route that moves
		# later must not quietly reopen this.
		#
		# This stops the ticket being WRITTEN here. It does not touch the `Referer`
		# header or the client's browser history, and the Odoo access log keeps its own
		# copy. Central log shipping is filtered separately, in promtail.
		format filter {
			wrap json
			fields {
				request>uri query {
					replace ticket REDACTED
				}
			}
		}
		level INFO
	}
}

